        {
          "id": "SP_THEONELLA_CONICA",
          "name": "Theonella conica",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "",
          "lineage": "Theonella",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "The live sponge is irregularly massive, lobate, with oscules either flush with the surface or opening upon mounds (Fig. 13 A – C). The color of the surface is brown while the body is blueish. The samples preserved in alcohol are ochre with an uneven surface, in some parts microhispid. The consistence is firm and slightly compressible. SKELETON. Ectosome consisting of phyllotriaenes and with a crust of numerous rhabds (Fig. 13 D – E). Choanosome formed by tetraclone desmas and brushes of strongyles (Fig. 13 F). SPICULES. Phyllotriaenes (Fig. 13 D) with short clads 40 – (57.5) – 87 µm (n = 8) and desmas quite rare; smooth and slightly curved strongyles (Fig. 13 G) 315 – (385, 54.3) – 417 µm × 2.5 – (3.25, 2.2) – 7 µm, sometimes modified in oxeas; rhabds (Fig. 13 H) are straight with oxeote ends and long spines 7 – (9.5) – 14 µm.",
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "wikipediaTitle": "gbif:2252346"
        },
